values (
  6,
  'Science',
  $bow$# Grade 6 Science — Budget of Work

## Term 1 (Q1: Materials — Changes of State, Mixtures, and Separation Techniques)
Describe changes of state (melting, evaporation, freezing, condensation) using diagrams and flowcharts, and explain the role of heat energy | Explain why physical changes are reversible and chemical changes are irreversible | Describe uniform and non-uniform mixtures (solutions and suspensions); describe air as a mixture of oxygen, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and water vapor | Demonstrate separation techniques — decantation, winnowing, scooping, picking, evaporation, filtering, sieving, and using magnets — and explain the benefit of each in preparing useful products | Apply the features of a fair test (change one factor, measure one factor, keep all others the same) and recognize that valid investigations need at least three trials with accurate measurement and recording.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner choose one real household mixture (sand and water, rice and beans, salt and sand) and apply the separation technique they judge most appropriate, then explain in writing why that technique — rather than an alternative — fit the mixture's properties.

## Term 2 (Q2: Living Things — Circulatory System, Plant Reproduction, Vertebrates/Invertebrates, Food Webs)
Identify the parts of the circulatory system (heart, blood, blood vessels) and describe how they work | Describe how plants reproduce — pollination, seed production, and plant propagation (cutting, budding, layering, grafting) | Plan a simple scientific investigation, applying the features of a fair test and replication, to determine which propagation method works best for a garden plant | Describe the difference between vertebrates and invertebrates using local examples | Describe the roles of producers, consumers, scavengers, and decomposers in a food web | Identify living things as biotic factors and light, water, temperature, and soil type as abiotic factors, and explain how their interactions can help or harm an ecosystem.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner apply a real fair test at home — growing two of the same plant cutting with only one factor different (e.g. amount of water) — and record which plant grows better and why, based on the controlled variable.

## Term 3 (Q3: Force, Motion, and Energy — Simple Machines and Waves)
Observe and describe examples and uses of simple machines (inclined planes, wedges, levers, pulleys) found at home, school, and in the community, and demonstrate their advantages and limitations through guided investigation | Carry out fair tests to show how levers change the magnitude and direction of a force | Identify that waves carry energy from a source to a receiver; investigate water waves in a tub or ripple tank and describe their shape (crests/troughs), size (width/height), and movement patterns | Identify differences between longitudinal and transverse waves, with examples of each, and describe how sound changes when the source or receiver is moving.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner design, build, and test a simple machine (e.g. a lever-based device) for a real task at home, then describe one specific change they made after testing it to improve how it worked.

## Term 4 (Q4: Earth and Space — Volcanic Activity, Seasons, Earth's Motions, Constellations)
Explain what volcanoes are and how they are formed; identify the nearest active/inactive volcano to the local community and assess eruption risk; interpret PHIVOLCS Alert Levels to explain what to do before, during, and after an eruption | Describe the different seasons in the Philippines and appropriate activities for each | Demonstrate Earth's rotation on its axis using a globe to explain day and night; build a Sun-Earth-Moon model to explain seasons, star patterns over a year, and solar/lunar eclipses | Explain why ancient cultures relied on constellations for direction and season-tracking, and gather information on how indigenous Filipino communities represented eclipses and night-sky patterns.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner track shadow length and direction from a fixed stick or object at the same time of day across at least a week, and use the real observed pattern to explain, in their own words, what it reveals about Earth's motion.$bow$,
  'https://eduksama.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/FINAL-MATATAG-Science-CG-2023-Grades-3-10.pdf (Grade 6 table, same combined Grades 3-10 document used for the Grade 3 source)'
);
